---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Chart from '../../components/Chart.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---
<Layout title="CEG Flyering Experiment" description="A Civic Engagement Group outreach case study by Monalisa Hota, combining creative flyer design, distribution strategy, and analysis of website activity in Arlington.">
  <section class="page-intro"><p class="eyebrow">Data analytics / case study</p><h1>A small flyer. A bigger question.</h1><p class="lead">Can a thoughtfully designed invitation bring more neighbours into civic life? An outreach experiment at the intersection of creativity, community, and evidence.</p><p><a class="text-link" href={`${base}analytics/`}>Back to data analytics</a></p><div class="tags"><span>Flyer design</span><span>Distribution strategy</span><span>Data analysis</span><span>Visual storytelling</span></div></section>
  <div class="case-meta"><div><span class="eyebrow">Organisation</span><p>Envision Arlington<br />Civic Engagement Group</p></div><div><span class="eyebrow">My involvement</span><p>December 2024 – May 2026</p></div><div><span class="eyebrow">The approach</span><p>Design → Distribute → Observe → Reflect</p></div></div>
  <section class="split"><div><p class="eyebrow">The challenge</p><h2>More voices. More possibilities.</h2></div><div><p class="lead">The Civic Engagement Group connects Arlington residents with opportunities to participate in the life of their town.</p><p>As part of the Envision Arlington Committee, Monalisa led an outreach experiment involving flyer design, distribution strategy, and data analysis. The materials tell the story of an invitation—and what happened to website activity around successive outreach efforts.</p><p>The analysis compares unique visitors and site sessions over time, using annotated visuals to surface patterns and questions.</p></div></section>
  <section class="feature split"><div><p class="eyebrow">01 / The creative invitation</p><h2>From ideas to action.</h2><p class="lead">“The CEG can help you transform your ideas into action.”</p><p>The flyer combines a welcoming message, recognisable Arlington imagery, a meeting invitation, and a QR code. It translates the group’s mission into a direct invitation for neighbours to get involved.</p><p>A strong visual hierarchy connects the community story with a practical next step: visit the CEG website and register.</p><a class="button" href={`${base}documents/ceg-outreach-flyer.pdf`}>Open the original flyer · PDF ↗</a></div><a class="flyer-preview" href={`${base}documents/ceg-outreach-flyer.pdf`}><img src={`${base}images/ceg/flyer.webp`} alt="Monalisa’s CEG outreach flyer: Arlington Neighbors, transform your ideas into action, join us on Zoom" width="1400" height="1082" loading="lazy" /></a></section>
  <section><div class="section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">02 / Following the evidence</p><h2>Three phases. Different signals.</h2></div><p>Website activity provided a way to examine the response around each outreach period.</p></div><div class="grid three timeline"><article><span class="phase-dot">01</span><p class="eyebrow">18 April – 13 May 2025</p><h3>Spring outreach</h3><p>The spring comparison shows higher website activity after outreach. Monthly views place the result alongside earlier years.</p></article><article><span class="phase-dot">02</span><p class="eyebrow">29 September – 18 October 2025</p><h3>Autumn outreach</h3><p>Distribution was incomplete: flyers were taken down. The chart notes postcards sent on 18 October and no apparent improvement.</p></article><article><span class="phase-dot">03</span><p class="eyebrow">13 February 2026</p><h3>Postcards only</h3><p>A 30-day before/after view shows fewer visitors afterward. Earlier February and March peaks provide important context.</p></article></div></section>
  <section class="evidence"><p class="eyebrow">The long view</p><h2>Put the moment in context.</h2><p class="measure-note"><strong>Reading the charts:</strong> bars represent unique visitors; red crosses represent site sessions, which can include repeat visits. Their axes are labelled separately. These are the original supplied visuals.</p><Chart image="all-months" title="Website activity across the full period" alt="Monthly unique visitors and site sessions from May 2023 to May 2026, with outreach and seasonal annotations" caption="The full timeline helps distinguish an outreach-period change from background variation. The annotations note that February and March have historically attracted higher traffic, possibly around town elections." /></section>
  <section class="evidence"><p class="eyebrow">Phase 01 / Spring 2025</p><h2>A promising signal.</h2><div class="finding"><strong>40 → 53</strong><p>Site sessions in the supplied spring before/after comparison.<br /><small>The chart uses 60-day windows and excludes March from the pre-outreach comparison.</small></p></div><Chart image="phase1-before-after" title="Spring outreach: before and after" alt="Spring 2025 comparison: site sessions rise from 40 before outreach to 53 afterward; unique visitors also increase" caption="The original chart reports more unique visitors and revisits after 18 April 2025. Its March exclusion means the comparison should be read with the stated seasonal adjustment in mind." /><Chart image="phase1-months" title="Spring outreach in its monthly context" alt="Monthly spring website traffic compared across years, with annotations about the 2025 outreach" caption="The companion monthly chart places spring outreach alongside prior years, adding context to the shorter before/after view." /></section>
  <section class="evidence"><p class="eyebrow">Phase 02 / Autumn 2025</p><h2>When delivery changes the experiment.</h2><p class="lead">An outreach idea is only as useful as its real-world delivery.</p><Chart image="phase2-months" title="Autumn outreach: incomplete distribution" alt="August to October traffic across 2022–2025; annotations describe removed flyers and incomplete autumn outreach" caption="The supplied annotation explains that flyers were taken down, with some remaining at Robbins Library and stores. Postcards were sent on 18 October. The chart does not show an apparent traffic improvement associated with this phase." /></section>
  <section class="evidence"><p class="eyebrow">Phase 03 / February 2026</p><h2>A different outcome. An equally useful lesson.</h2><div class="finding amber"><strong>30 → 18</strong><p>Unique visitors in the 30-day before/after comparison.<br /><small>After the postcard-only outreach on 13 February 2026.</small></p></div><Chart image="phase3-before-after" title="Postcards only: before and after" alt="Postcard-only comparison shows 30 unique visitors before 13 February 2026 and 18 afterward, with fewer site sessions too" caption="The post-outreach window shows fewer unique visitors and site sessions. This phase did not show the positive pattern seen in the spring comparison." /><Chart image="phase3-months" title="Postcards only in seasonal context" alt="January to March website activity across 2022–2026; annotation notes historically high February and March traffic" caption="The monthly view highlights that this time of year has previously seen organically higher traffic. February’s monthly total and the post-outreach 30-day count cover different windows and should not be treated as the same measure." /></section>
  <section class="reflection split"><div><p class="eyebrow">03 / What the experiment teaches</p><h2>Good analysis makes room for the unexpected.</h2></div><div><h3>Creativity opens the door.</h3><p>The flyer makes a civic invitation tangible, recognisable, and actionable.</p><h3>Context makes the evidence useful.</h3><p>Seasonality, incomplete distribution, and different comparison windows all matter when interpreting changes in traffic.</p><h3>Mixed results can guide the next question.</h3><p>Spring’s positive pattern and the later weaker responses offer a basis for refining outreach. These descriptive comparisons do not isolate a causal effect or measure meeting attendance.</p><p class="small">Source: the supplied CEG flyer and six annotated chart images. No underlying dataset was supplied; figures quoted here are taken from chart labels. A future round could track distribution counts, campaign-specific QR links, and registrations alongside website activity.</p></div></section>
  <dialog class="chart-dialog" aria-labelledby="chart-title"><div class="dialog-header"><h2 id="chart-title">Chart detail</h2><button type="button" class="close-dialog" autofocus>Close <span aria-hidden="true">×</span></button></div><div class="dialog-image"><img alt="" /></div><p class="small">On smaller screens, scroll horizontally to read the original chart.</p></dialog>
  <script>
    const dialog = document.querySelector<HTMLDialogElement>('.chart-dialog');
    const image = dialog?.querySelector('img');
    const title = dialog?.querySelector('#chart-title');
    document.querySelectorAll<HTMLAnchorElement>('.chart-zoom').forEach(link => {
      link.addEventListener('click', event => {
        if (!dialog || !image || !title || event.ctrlKey || event.metaKey || event.shiftKey || event.altKey) return;
        event.preventDefault();
        image.src = link.href;
        image.alt = link.querySelector('img')?.alt ?? '';
        title.textContent = link.dataset.caption ?? 'Chart detail';
        dialog.showModal();
      });
    });
    dialog?.querySelector('button')?.addEventListener('click', () => dialog.close());
    dialog?.addEventListener('click', event => { if (event.target === dialog) dialog.close(); });
  </script>
</Layout>
